Let’s reason through this step by step.

**Entity types in the schema**  
The schema describes vessels (ships) with static identifiers (MMSI, IMO, ship_id, name, type, flag, dimensions, year built, etc.) and dynamic position/status data (lat, lon, speed, heading, course, timestamp, etc.). There are also port-related fields (current_port, last_port, next_port with IDs, UN/LOCODEs, names, countries) and voyage data (destination, ETA, distances, speeds).  

The main entity is clearly a **Vessel** (a type of Vehicle, which is Equipment, Materiel, Object, BattlespaceConcept). Each vessel has a unique MMSI, IMO, and ship_id. MMSI is the most universal AIS identifier, present in every record, so it will be the subject identifier.

**Ports** appear as separate entities: current_port, last_port, next_port each have an ID, UN/LOCODE, name, and country. The ontology has `Port` (subclass of MaritimeTransportFacility, Facility, Object, BattlespaceConcept). These should be separate subject mappings, not literals on the vessel.

**Geographic position** (lat, lon) is a dynamic observation point. The ontology has `AbsolutePoint` (subclass of Point, Geometry) with latitude and longitude. This can be linked from the vessel via `hasGeographicLocation` (from BattlespaceConcept) or from an Observation via `hasGeometry`. Since the timestamp and position come together, an `Observation` entity with `observationDateTime` and a `hasGeometry` pointing to an `AbsolutePoint` is appropriate. The vessel can then have `hasObservation` (from Vehicle) pointing to the Observation.

**Velocity** (speed, course) and **Orientation** (heading) are separate concepts in the ontology. The vessel can have `hasVelocity` and `hasOrientation` properties (unattached, but usable from Vehicle via the general pattern). These will be literal values.

**Affiliation** – the `flag` column gives a country code. The ontology has `GeopoliticalAffiliation` with `geopoliticalAffiliationCode`. The vessel can have `hasAffiliation` pointing to a GeopoliticalAffiliation entity.

**Alternate identifiers** – IMO is an alternate identifier. The ontology has `AlternateIdentifier` with `alternateIdentifierStandardCode` and `alternateIdentifierValue`. The vessel can have `hasAlternateIdentifier` pointing to an AlternateIdentifier entity.

**Type name** – `type_name` maps to `bsm:typeName` on Object (and thus Vessel).

**Destination** – a literal string, but the ontology has no direct property for destination on Vessel. It could be considered an action description or part of a voyage, but there is no suitable property. It will be unmapped.

**ETA** – no suitable property in the ontology for Vessel. Unmapped.

**Dimensions** (length, width, draught, l_fore, w_left) – no direct properties in the ontology for Vessel. Unmapped.

**Year built** – no property. Unmapped.

**GRT, DWT** – no property. Unmapped.

**Speed values** (speed, avg_speed, max_speed) – `speed` is instantaneous speed (knots, but we map as literal). `vesselMaximumSpeed` exists on Vessel, but that is a design maximum, not current speed. `max_speed` could be that. `avg_speed` is not in ontology. `speed` (current) could map to `bsm:speedMetersPerSecond` on Velocity, but the unit is knots, not m/s. We’ll map it as a literal with low confidence, noting unit mismatch.

**Course** – maps to `absoluteBearingAngle` on Velocity.

**Heading** – maps to `headingAngle` on Orientation.

**Timestamp** – maps to `observationDateTime` on Observation.

**Status** – numeric code, no suitable property. Unmapped.

**Market** – no property. Unmapped.

